# swift-cross-ui v0.8.0 GtkBackend Windows main-loop starvation fix (CUESYNC-9)
#
# Pinned to tag v0.8.0, commit a6d206370812e3b9edba259d167e848892c5013d (matches
# Package.swift's `exact: "0.8.0"` / Package.resolved). Applied by `git apply` to the
# *resolved checkout* at `.build/checkouts/swift-cross-ui` — the upstream dependency
# source is never edited in place in this repo; this file is the only checked-in copy
# of the change. Kept SEPARATE from patches/swift-cross-ui-0.8.0-gtk-interactivity.patch
# (CUESYNC-8) so each patch documents exactly one root cause; apply the interactivity
# patch first, this one second (see the CI workflow / scripts/patch-swift-cross-ui.sh
# ordering). Both patches touch Sources/GtkBackend/GtkBackend.swift, but at disjoint
# line ranges (the CUESYNC-8 Shape-widget factory vs. the run-loop tickler here), so
# they apply cleanly in sequence.
#
# Root cause (spec CUESYNC-9 §2/§3, specs/CUESYNC-9-findings.md §0/§2.5): Fork W — the
# whole Windows window is inert (scrollbars, TextFields, close button) AND, per the
# live click-probe evidence, renders essentially empty and size-collapsed. Both halves
# are one defect: GtkBackend's `mainRunLoopTicklingLoop` (added upstream in PR #141 to
# make `@MainActor`/`DispatchQueue.main` work under GtkBackend off-Apple) ticks
# Foundation's `RunLoop.main` on Windows too. swift-corelibs-foundation unconditionally
# binds `RunLoop.main` to this thread's Win32 message queue on Windows
# (`_CFRunLoopSetWindowsMessageQueueMask(_, QS_ALLINPUT, ...)` in RunLoop.swift's
# `_mainRunLoop`, "similar to AppKit and UIKit") — the very same queue GDK's own win32
# backend drains for input AND its frame-clock repaint/relayout via this GLib main loop.
# CoreFoundation's Windows run-loop wait calls `PeekMessage(NULL, ...)` + `DispatchMessage`
# on every tick, and the tickler busy-reschedules at 0 ms whenever `limitDate` returns a
# past/now date (there is almost always a ready libdispatch item), so Foundation wakes
# the GLib context continuously and monopolizes the one thread-global queue GDK needs to
# BOTH paint and dispatch input — the empty, dead-input window the probe captured.
#
# Fix (three coordinated hunks, all `#if os(Windows)`, all GLib's own public APIs — no
# new dependency, no dynamic load, no text-substitution, no GtkFixed/absolute-position):
#   1. Schedule the tickler's `g_timeout_add_full` at G_PRIORITY_DEFAULT_IDLE (200),
#      BELOW GDK_PRIORITY_EVENTS (== G_PRIORITY_DEFAULT == 0) and GDK_PRIORITY_REDRAW
#      (== G_PRIORITY_HIGH_IDLE + 20 == 120). GLib dispatches only sources at or above
#      the highest-priority ready source each iteration, so whenever GDK has a pending
#      input message or a queued repaint, the tickler is skipped that frame and GDK —
#      not Foundation's competing PeekMessage/DispatchMessage — owns the win32 queue.
#      This is the load-bearing fix: it structurally stops Foundation from ever
#      preempting GDK's own queue drain, instead of racing it.
#   2. Floor the reschedule delay at 8 ms (~120 Hz) so the tickler can never busy-loop
#      at 0 ms and wake the GLib main context every iteration (100% CPU + starvation).
#   3. Drain GLib's own default `GMainContext` non-blockingly first, every tick
#      (`while g_main_context_iteration(nil, 0) != 0 {}`), so GDK processes whatever is
#      already pending before Foundation's pass — belt-and-suspenders reinforcement of
#      (1) within a single tick.
# PR #141's `@MainActor`/`DispatchQueue.main` servicing is preserved (the tickler still
# runs, just below GDK and off the 0 ms busy path) — CueSync's own
# `.task { state.loadPreferences() }` depends on it. Linux/macOS behaviour is
# byte-identical to upstream (the `#else` branches keep G_PRIORITY_DEFAULT and the
# 0 ms floor; macOS never runs the tickler at all).
#
# Idempotency: `.github/workflows/swift-windows.yml` and scripts/patch-swift-cross-ui.sh
# guard re-application with `git apply --reverse --check` before applying, so a second
# run against an already-patched checkout is a no-op rather than a failure.
